<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Patch>

	<!-- Enrols the pomeranian in A Dog Said... Animal Prosthetics 2.

	     ADS 2 does not name recipes one by one any more. It keeps three abstract RecipeDefs,
	     ADS_Cat1 (medieval replacements), ADS_Cat2 (+ simple prosthetics) and ADS_Cat3
	     (+ bionics), and its own last patch - Patches/z_Category_Patches.xml, named z_ so it
	     sorts after everything else in the mod - copies their recipeUsers onto the real
	     surgery bases with PatchOperationAddOrMergeCopy. Adding an animal therefore means
	     adding one <li> to those three lists, which is what every ModCompat file in ADS 2
	     does. See 1.6/ModCompat/Dinosauria/Patches/Category_Patches_Dinosauria.xml.

	     All three, because a pomeranian is a trainable pet: ADS 2's own settings text puts
	     "pack animals and trainable pets" in category 3, and the lists are cumulative, so a
	     category-3 animal appears in all of them. That is where vanilla's Husky,
	     LabradorRetriever and YorkshireTerrier are.

	     The guard is PatchOperationConditional on ADS_Cat1. Without ADS 2 the xpath selects
	     nothing, no <nomatch> is declared, and the operation simply reports success - no error
	     line, nothing logged.

	     NOT MayRequire. MayRequire on an <Operation> is read by nothing: Verse.PatchOperation
	     has no such field, and ModContentPack.LoadPatches - which builds the operations - never
	     reads the attribute. The operation would apply whether ADS 2 is loaded or not. The
	     attribute IS honoured on a def node, on a <li> and on any ordinary field element, which
	     is what makes the wrong form look exactly like the right one; live 1.6 mods ship it.
	     There is no native way to gate an operation on a packageId, so this tests for what the
	     mod declares instead. PatchOperationFindMod is the other option and compares a display
	     name, which is a different string again.

	     Every branch of the predicate repeats @Name=. The form [@Name="A" or "B"] is not the
	     test it looks like: XPath converts each operand of `or` to a boolean and a non-empty
	     string literal is true, so that predicate is true for every RecipeDef in the document.
	     That is the bug this rewrite exists to remove. -->
	<Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
		<xpath>/Defs/RecipeDef[@Name="ADS_Cat1"]</xpath>
		<match Class="PatchOperationAdd">
			<xpath>/Defs/RecipeDef[
				@Name="ADS_Cat3" or
				@Name="ADS_Cat2" or
				@Name="ADS_Cat1"
			]/recipeUsers</xpath>
			<value>
				<li>aka_Pomeranian</li>
			</value>
		</match>
	</Operation>

</Patch>
